# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env (see .gitignore).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — two connection strings, per ADR-004.
#
#   MIGRATION role : owns the schema, runs Prisma migrations.
#   APP role       : the runtime connection. NOSUPERUSER, NOBYPASSRLS, owns nothing.
#
# These MUST be different roles. Pointing DATABASE_URL at the migration role
# disables tenant isolation entirely while every structural test still passes —
# the catalog suite asserts against exactly that mistake.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://meterlog_app:app_dev_password@localhost:5432/meterlog?schema=public"
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://meterlog_migrator:migrator_dev_password@localhost:5432/meterlog?schema=public"

# Consumed by docker-compose to seed the local cluster's roles.
POSTGRES_DB=meterlog
POSTGRES_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=migrator_dev_password
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=app_dev_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redis — session store (ADR-001). Hard runtime dependency, not just a cache.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3001
# Secret used to sign the session cookie. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET="replace-me-with-32-bytes-of-hex"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web
#
# `API_ORIGIN` — the origin the Next rewrite forwards `/api/*` to
# (apps/web/next.config.mjs). It is the one variable the whole cross-origin
# cookie posture rests on (ADR-001's amendment), and every way of getting it
# wrong fails SILENTLY. Four rules, each with the failure it prevents:
#
#   1. IT IS READ AT BUILD TIME, NOT ONLY AT RUNTIME. `rewrites()` is evaluated
#      when Next loads its config, and `next build` bakes the result into the
#      routing manifest. Set it as a runtime-only variable on Vercel and the
#      DEPLOYED manifest carries next.config.mjs's `?? 'http://localhost:3001'`
#      fallback instead: every API call fails in production with nothing in the
#      repo wrong and nothing in the build log to read.
#   2. IT IS NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, deliberately. It is read server-side, inside
#      `rewrites()`. A public copy would put the API origin in the browser
#      bundle, which is how a direct browser-to-API call gets written — and that
#      call is cross-SITE, so the `SameSite=Lax` session cookie is not sent and
#      login appears to succeed and then 401s.
#   3. NO TRAILING PATH. The rewrite appends `/api/:path*` itself, so a value
#      ending in `/api/v1` yields `/api/v1/api/v1/...` and 404s everywhere.
#   4. `https://` IN PRODUCTION. The session cookie is `Secure` there
#      (apps/api/src/auth/auth.controller.ts), and a `Secure` cookie does not
#      survive a plain-HTTP hop. Local is plain HTTP precisely because the local
#      cookie is not `Secure`.
#
# In production this is the Render API origin, set on Vercel by hand — it is an
# author action, not a committed value. See `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §16.
#
# NOTHING ELSE BELONGS IN THIS SECTION. `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` stood here until
# the deploy-config PR and was read by no code at all; it is deleted rather than
# left inert because, unlike the `CORS_ORIGIN` leftover it resembles, ACTING on
# its name — pointing the browser at the API — is exactly the failure rule 2
# describes. Do not reintroduce it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
API_ORIGIN="http://localhost:3001"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability — wired at build-order step 10 (the observability PR).
#
# BLANK IS A WORKING CONFIGURATION, and deliberately so: no DSN means Sentry is
# off, which is what local development and CI want. A test run must not depend
# on an external service, and nobody should need an account to run the app. The
# API logs which state it is in once at boot rather than leaving it ambiguous.
#
# TWO DSN VARIABLES, AND THE `NEXT_PUBLIC_` ONE IS CORRECT — which is worth a
# line, because the deploy-config PR deleted a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable and
# argued hard that `API_ORIGIN` must never become one. The rule is about what an
# exposure ENABLES, not about the prefix. A Sentry DSN is a public, write-only
# identifier: it lets the holder SEND events to a project and read nothing, and
# a browser SDK cannot function without it in the bundle. An API origin in the
# bundle enables a direct browser-to-API call, which makes the session cookie
# cross-site and breaks login. Recorded in ADR-020.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SENTRY_DSN=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=""

# pino's level. Read by the API since the observability PR; before that it sat
# here unread. `debug` locally, `info` in production when unset — and `silent`
# under NODE_ENV=test regardless, so the suite is not buried in request logs.
LOG_LEVEL=debug
